# Module-wide options, all LANTERN_*.

menu "Lantern"

config LANTERN_KEYS
    bool "Paint the key LEDs from the palette, the strip and the keys from the lantern,keys node"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_KEYS_ENABLED
    select LED_STRIP
    select ZMK_LOW_PRIORITY_WORK_QUEUE
    help
      On a device with a lantern,keys node (a split half of the Cyboard
      Imprint in canon): the module is the one writer of the node's strip
      (src/keys.c). Each LED shows the picture the choice LANTERN_PICTURE
      picks, by default the bands: the palette slot the node assigns to the
      key under it (positions: the key under each LED, in chain order;
      slots: the slot of each key position, every key slot 0 without it),
      so the palette's colours fall on the keyboard in bands. A slot that
      is 0x000000 shows slot 0, and a
      device without a palette shows white (LANTERN_NO_PICTURE's black
      aside). A frame is written when the LEDs
      light or go dark (LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER) and when the palette changes,
      on ZMK's low priority work queue, which this selects.

      CONFIG_ZMK_RGB_UNDERGLOW must be n on such a device (asserted): its
      tick would repaint the strip. The strip's chain-length must be the LED
      count, one positions entry each, and slots, where given, one entry per
      key position of the keymap (both asserted). Default y where the node is
      present: the node is the switch, and a target without one (a dongle)
      builds none of the painting.

config LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Brightness of the key LEDs, in percent of full scale: the picture's unless LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS is lower, and the base of the frame's cap"
    range 1 100
    default 16
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      Every palette colour is scaled so that its brightest channel is a
      percentage of full scale (255), which keeps its hue and saturation, a
      grey or black scaling to white: this one, unless
      LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS sets the picture lower. Whatever the
      picture, a frame draws at most every LED white at this percentage plus
      two keys white at full scale (src/keys.c). 16 is what ZMK's underglow
      drove on canon's halves before this module painted the strip
      (brightness 25 between Cyboard's 5 and 50).

config LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Brightness of the picture alone, in percent of full scale, at most LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS"
    range 1 LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    default LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      The picture's colours, the countdown's other pictures and the stain's
      dye at this percentage of full scale instead of LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS, so
      that the picture can be dimmer than the effects over it: an effect
      still brightens to full scale and a frame's cap stays the one
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS sets, so a dimmer picture leaves the effects as much
      room or more. Colours that paint alike at this brightness count once,
      so a dimmer picture can merge two close colours: one picture fewer in
      the countdown and one colour fewer in an effect's cycle. Default
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS; never above it (asserted: a still picture could
      pass the cap). canon t-skdt tries half of it under the disc.

config LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    bool "Light the key LEDs while USB powers this half, dark on battery"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      The rule the key LEDs follow (src/on_usb_power.c): while USB powers
      the device and it is in use (LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE), the LEDs show the
      palette's picture (LANTERN_KEYS); on battery they are black (and
      unpowered with LANTERN_RAIL, which is what saves the battery); the
      boot light (LANTERN_INTROS) lights them at
      boot and when USB power arrives whatever the rule says. With a
      lantern,rail node the rule switches the LEDs' supply too
      (LANTERN_RAIL); without one the supply is the board's (ZMK's
      ext_power driver's, or a rail always on) and dark is a black frame.

      Needs CONFIG_USB_DEVICE_STACK (ZMK's default on nRF52840, the split
      peripheral included) and CONFIG_SETTINGS; the source asserts each at
      build time, so a wrong conf fails the build with a message. Default y
      with the keys, which nothing else lights (src/keys.c asserts it); a
      target without a lantern,keys node (a dongle) builds none of it.

config LANTERN_RAIL
    bool "Switch the key LEDs' supply with the rule: the lantern,rail node's pin"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_RAIL_ENABLED
    depends on GPIO
    help
      On a device whose LEDs' supply has an enable pin (a split half of the
      Cyboard Imprint in canon): the rail is on while the rule lights the
      LEDs and off while they are dark, so on battery they draw nothing at
      all (the rail alone drew 10-18 mA a half at rest on the Imprint). The
      rail is the node's gpios, the pin ZMK's zmk,ext-power-generic node
      would drive as control-gpios; that node stays disabled in the
      consumer's overlay (src/on_usb_power.c says why), and a gpio hog may
      hold the pin low from reset until the module takes it; the node's
      settle-ms passes between the rail and the first frame. Default y
      where the node is present: the node is the switch. =n with the node
      present compiles every pin write out, so a hog that holds the pin low
      keeps the strip unpowered: drop the node and the hog instead.

config LANTERN_INTROS
    bool
    help
      An intro is built (src/intros/, one file an intro; the table
      src/intro.h): the boot light, which lights the key LEDs at boot
      whatever the power and whenever USB power arrives, and plays the picked
      intro for its length (LANTERN_INTRO, the choice LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT)
      before LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER's rule applies. Each intro selects it;
      nothing to set. Without one there is no boot light.

config LANTERN_INTRO_BANDS
    bool "Build the bands: the palette's bands for a second, as the boot light"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    select LANTERN_INTROS
    help
      The intro bands (src/intro.h), built into the image and played when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_INTRO, LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT): the palette's
      bands, each key its slot's colour at the picture's brightness, for a
      second (src/intros/bands.c) at boot, cable or not, and whenever USB
      power arrives, so that a half shows it has power whatever its picture
      (LANTERN_NO_PICTURE paints every key black); then LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER's
      rule applies. Default y: the boot light a consumer has without a line.
      With no other intro built, =n drops the boot light, and the rule
      applies at once.

config LANTERN_INTRO_STAR_WAVES
    bool "Build the star waves: ten seconds in red, blue and green from the outer keys to the thumbs and back, as the boot light"
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    select LANTERN_INTROS
    select LANTERN_FRAMES
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The intro star-waves (src/intro.h), built into the image and played
      when the pick names it (LANTERN_INTRO, LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT): the boot
      light plays a ten-second show (src/intros/star_waves.c tells it) in the
      bands' place, at boot, cable or not, and whenever USB power arrives,
      into an idle half as well; then LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER's rule applies, as
      after any boot light. Nothing cuts it short, and power that arrives
      while it plays does not restart it. Each half plays it for itself,
      mirrored, from its outermost key to its innermost thumb and back,
      measured on the keys' places (LANTERN_GEOMETRY: the lantern,keys
      node's coords, or the physical layout's). Its colours are
      its own, not the palette's: red, blue and green up to full scale under
      the frame's cap, and no other, white included, a key in one of the
      three at a time (canon t-5v1f and t-ky8x: the user's pick, after
      Pokemon's first games). A key pressed while it plays shows no effect.
      Default n: a consumer builds the intros it wants to pick from; a conf
      that builds this one gets it as the default (LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT).

config LANTERN_INTRO
    bool "Take the pick of the intro from the split central's host, and keep it across boots"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_INTRO_ENABLED
    depends on SETTINGS
    select LANTERN_PICK
    help
      Which of the intros built into a half's image the boot light plays,
      chosen at run time: the host hands the split central a pick, one intro
      by name (LANTERN_PORT), the central invokes the lantern,behavior-intro
      node, which ZMK's split transport carries to every half by its name
      (the node is named intro on every device, src/intro.c, the binding),
      and the central and every half keep the pick in their settings
      ("lantern/intro/mask"). A half plays the picked intro when it builds
      it; with no pick stored, or the picked intro not built, the one
      LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT names. On a split central the pick is resent to a
      half at each of its battery reports, with the palette and the effects'
      pick (src/pick.c; the run queue must hold one more invocation a half,
      LANTERN_EFFECT says). canon's scripts/dongle.py intro sends it
      (t-qvgv). Default y where the node is present: the node is the switch,
      as the palette's.

choice LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT
    prompt "The intro the boot light plays while no pick is stored, or the picked intro is not built"
    depends on LANTERN_INTROS
    default LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT_STAR_WAVES if LANTERN_INTRO_STAR_WAVES
    help
      One of the intros built into the image (each member depends on its
      intro): what the boot light plays before the host's first pick
      (src/intro.c) and whenever the stored pick names no intro this image
      builds, and the only pick a consumer without a host has. Without a
      line: the star waves when they are built (a conf that asks for the
      show wants to see it), else the bands. canon's halves write the line.

config LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT_BANDS
    bool "The bands"
    depends on LANTERN_INTRO_BANDS

config LANTERN_INTRO_DEFAULT_STAR_WAVES
    bool "The star waves"
    depends on LANTERN_INTRO_STAR_WAVES

endchoice

config LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE
    bool "Dark once ZMK's activity state leaves ACTIVE, lit again on activity"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    help
      While USB powers the device, the LEDs go dark (and the rail off, with
      LANTERN_RAIL), as on battery, once ZMK turns IDLE: CONFIG_ZMK_IDLE_TIMEOUT
      ms (ZMK's default 30000) without a key, sensor or pointing input on
      this device, each split half for itself. The next input lights them
      again (settle-ms after the rail, with one). The boot light is not cut short. The timeout is the
      consumer's line (canon's halves set 300000); a cable plugged into an
      idle device plays the boot light's intro (LANTERN_INTROS) and goes dark
      at its end, and lights nothing else until its next input (the source
      says why).

config LANTERN_COUNTDOWN
    bool "Count down to the idle dark in the palette's colours, a stage a colour"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE
    depends on LANTERN_PALETTE
    help
      While the key LEDs are lit, each half counts the time since its last key
      in stages of LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS and changes the picture at each:
      the bands, then the whole half in each colour of the palette in turn (a
      cleared slot is slot 0's colour; colours that paint alike at
      LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS count once), then the bands again, until ZMK
      turns idle and LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE blanks the LEDs. A key brings the
      bands back and restarts the clock; a cable plugged in lights the stage
      the keys say (src/keys.c). A sensor or pointing input counts for ZMK's
      idle and not for this clock. One colour: the bands until the dark.

config LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS
    int "Milliseconds between two pictures of the countdown"
    range 100 3600000
    default 10000
    depends on LANTERN_COUNTDOWN
    help
      How long each picture of the countdown shows before the next: the
      bands, then each colour of the palette, then the bands again, until
      ZMK's idle blanks the LEDs. Ten seconds is what canon's user chose on
      hardware (2026-10-20, t-x82q); a minute a colour, with the colours
      running out at the idle timeout, read as still.

choice LANTERN_PICTURE
    prompt "The key LEDs' picture, under the countdown and the effects"
    default LANTERN_BANDS
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      What the LEDs show while lit, the countdown's first picture and the
      one the effects draw over (src/keys.c), or every key black
      (LANTERN_NO_PICTURE). The bands by default; canon t-skdt tries the
      others one image at a time.

config LANTERN_BANDS
    bool "Each key in the slot the lantern,keys node's slots give it"
    help
      Each LED shows the palette slot the node's slots table gives the key
      under it, so the palette's colours fall on the keyboard in the bands
      the consumer draws (canon: five, slots 0..4 left to right).

config LANTERN_SOLID
    bool "Every key in slot 0, the heaviest colour"
    help
      Every LED shows slot 0, the whole half in one colour. The countdown's
      pictures are then the whole half in each distinct colour in turn
      (colours that paint alike count once), slot 0's first. The node keeps
      slots, which this picture does not read (the crossing ring and the
      beams do).

config LANTERN_GRADIENT
    bool "The palette's colours blended across the board from left to right, by each key's x"
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      Each LED shows a blend of the palette's colours by its key's place on
      the whole board (LANTERN_GEOMETRY: the lantern,keys node's coords, or
      the physical layout's): the leftmost key in
      the first distinct colour, the rightmost in the last, and each key
      between them the two colours around its place mixed in proportion, in
      slot order, so no two neighbours meet at an edge. A blend's brightest
      channel is the picture's brightness (LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS), as a
      colour's (a blend of two hues
      lights more channels, so it can look brighter). The halves share the
      board: the left half shows the first stretch, the right half the last.
      A colour shows pure only on a key at its place; no key sits between
      the halves, so a colour whose place lies there never does. The
      countdown's other pictures are as with the bands. Needs the keys'
      places (asserted). The
      node's slots, if any, this picture does not read (the crossing ring
      and the beams do).

config LANTERN_SCATTER
    bool "Throw the palette's slots over the keys at random, by the node's weights, a new throw every LANTERN_SCATTER_MS"
    depends on ENTROPY_HAS_DRIVER || TEST_RANDOM_GENERATOR
    help
      The palette's slots thrown over the half's LEDs at random: each slot
      on as many keys as its share of the lantern,keys node's
      scatter-weights (one percentage per slot, at most 100 in all,
      asserted; slots 1.. take their rounded share, slot 0, the heaviest
      colour, the rest), thrown again every LANTERN_SCATTER_MS while the
      LEDs are lit, keys or no keys, and at every light. Each half throws
      for itself. The node keeps slots, which this picture does not read
      (the crossing ring and the beams do).
      Needs a random number source (Zephyr's sys_rand32_get: an entropy
      driver, as the nRF52840's RNG, or the test generator).

config LANTERN_NO_PICTURE
    bool "No picture: every key black while lit, so that only the effects light"
    help
      Every LED black while the LEDs are lit, so that only the effects
      show, over black keys (and the stain's dye, which stays at the
      picture's brightness as over any picture). The boot light shows the
      picked intro, by default the bands (LANTERN_INTRO_BANDS), so that a
      half still shows it has power; on USB the keys turn black when it
      ends. With the
      countdown on, its first picture is the black one and the others the
      whole half in a colour, as with the bands, from
      LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS after the last key; canon's product images
      carry it with LANTERN_COUNTDOWN=n (t-skdt). The node keeps slots, which
      the bands intro reads and the crossing ring for its colours.

endchoice

config LANTERN_SCATTER_MS
    int "Milliseconds between two throws of the scatter"
    range 1000 3600000
    default 10000
    depends on LANTERN_SCATTER
    help
      How long each throw of the scatter shows before the next, while the
      LEDs are lit. Ten seconds is canon's user's first pick (t-skdt).

config LANTERN_FRAMES
    bool
    help
      The moving frames in src/keys.c: a frame every 30 ms while something
      moves, and the cap on a frame's draw. The effects' engine and a moving
      intro (the star waves) select it; nothing to set.

config LANTERN_EFFECTS
    bool
    select LANTERN_FRAMES
    help
      The effects' engine in src/keys.c: the ring of the half's last
      presses and the pull from the picture toward an effect's colour, on
      the moving frames and their cap (LANTERN_FRAMES), the table of the
      built effects (one file an effect under src/effects/, src/frame.h;
      each lives its design's time after a press, or on a larger board the
      time its shape needs to cross it, measured from the lantern,keys
      node's coords; the beams keep the design's fade) and the pick of
      which of them draw (LANTERN_EFFECT,
      the choice LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT). Each effect selects it; nothing
      to set.

config LANTERN_PICK
    bool
    help
      The picks' mechanism (src/pick.c): a value the split central's host
      chooses, kept by every device in its settings and resent to a half
      that is back. The pick of the effects (LANTERN_EFFECT) and the pick of
      the intro (LANTERN_INTRO) are its instances and select it; nothing to
      set.

config LANTERN_EFFECT
    bool "Take the pick of the effects from the split central's host, and keep it across boots"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_EFFECT_ENABLED
    depends on SETTINGS
    select LANTERN_PICK
    help
      Which of the effects built into a half's image draw, and in which
      colour, chosen at run time: the host hands the split central a pick,
      one or more of the effects by name and a colour or none (LANTERN_PORT),
      or a key of the central's keymap bound to the node does (`&effect
      <mask> <colour>`; LANTERN_EFFECT_NEXT cycles the mask from a key), the
      central invokes the lantern,behavior-effect node, which ZMK's split
      transport carries to every half by its name (the node is named effect
      on every device, src/effect.c, the binding), and the central and every
      half keep the pick in their settings ("lantern/effect/mask": the mask,
      then the colour). A half draws the picked effects it builds, in
      the pick's colour or without one in the palette's; with no pick
      stored, or none of its effects built, it draws the one
      LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT names, in LANTERN_EFFECT_COLOUR. On a split
      central the pick is resent to a half at each of its battery reports,
      with the palette (src/palette.c says why that signal), so a half that
      was away gets it when it is back; the run queue must then hold the
      palette's three invocations, this one and the intro's pick
      (LANTERN_INTRO) for every half at once,
      CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_SPLIT_RUN_QUEUE_SIZE at least 5 x the
      peripheral count with all three (10 for two halves; src/pick.c asserts
      the sum). canon's scripts/dongle.py effect sends the pick (t-5hqx), and
      changes the effect of the day without flashing. Default y where the
      node is present: the node is the switch, as the palette's.

choice LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT
    prompt "The effect a half draws while no pick is stored, or none of the pick's effects is built"
    depends on LANTERN_EFFECTS
    default LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_STAIN if LANTERN_STAIN
    help
      One of the effects built into the image (each member depends on its
      effect): what a half shows before the host's first pick (src/effect.c)
      and whenever the stored pick names no effect this image builds, and
      the only pick a consumer without a host has. canon's is the crossing
      ring (t-5hqx). Without a line: the stain when it is built (it builds
      the thin ring too, and a conf that asks for the stain wants the dye;
      Opus review 2026-10-26), else the first built effect in this order. A
      conf that builds several effects for a keyboard without a host writes
      the line.

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_THIN_RING
    bool "The thin ring"
    depends on LANTERN_THIN_RING

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_DISC
    bool "The disc"
    depends on LANTERN_DISC

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_STAIN
    bool "The stain, the thin ring that dyes"
    depends on LANTERN_STAIN

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_CROSS_RING
    bool "The crossing ring"
    depends on LANTERN_CROSS_RING

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_BEAMS
    bool "The beams"
    depends on LANTERN_BEAMS

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_WATER
    bool "The water"
    depends on LANTERN_WATER

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_DART
    bool "The dart"
    depends on LANTERN_DART

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_LAYER_KEYS
    bool "The layer keys"
    depends on LANTERN_LAYER_KEYS

endchoice

config LANTERN_EFFECT_COLOUR
    hex "The effects' colour while no pick is stored, 0xRRGGBB; 0 = the palette's colours"
    range 0 0xFFFFFF
    default 0
    depends on LANTERN_EFFECTS || LANTERN_EFFECT_NEXT
    help
      The one colour every effect draws in before the host's first pick
      (src/keys.c), and the only colour a consumer without a host has
      unless a key sets another (the effect behavior's second parameter,
      LANTERN_EFFECT). 0, the default, leaves the effects their own colours
      from the palette: the thin ring and the stain the next colour a press,
      the disc all of them in turn, the crossing ring and the beams the
      pressed key's band, the water and the dart the first to the last,
      the layer keys the slot of the layer's colour. A
      pick from the host or a key carries its own colour (0 for the
      palette's) and this is not read again, except by LANTERN_EFFECT_NEXT's
      first press with no pick stored, which keeps it: on a split central
      that builds no effects (a dongle) set it to the halves' value, since
      the central cannot read theirs.

config LANTERN_EFFECT_NEXT
    bool "Cycle the effects' pick from a key of the central's keymap: the lantern,behavior-effect-next node"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_EFFECT_NEXT_ENABLED
    depends on LANTERN_EFFECT
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    help
      A key bound to the lantern,behavior-effect-next node (`&effect_next`
      in the keymap) picks, on press, the effect after the stored pick in
      LANTERN_EFFECT_LIST's order, over the effects this central builds, or
      the whole table where it builds none (a dongle), round again after the
      last, and keeps the pick's colour (src/effect_next.c); with no pick
      stored, the effect after LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT where this central
      builds effects (a dongle: the table's first) in LANTERN_EFFECT_COLOUR.
      The pick goes to every half through the effect behavior, as the
      host's does. For a keyboard without a host; `&effect <mask> <colour>`
      on a key is the fixed pick. The behavior runs on the central only,
      where the keymap runs, and the send is a split central's, hence the
      dependency. Default y where the node is present on a split central.
      Neither key binding is verified on hardware (canon's keymap binds
      none; the check build compiles both).

config LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    bool
    help
      The board's geometry for what measures it (src/frame.h): each key's
      place in hundredths of a key unit, the lantern,keys node's coords (an
      x and a y per key position, asserted twice the key count) or without
      them the keyboard's chosen zmk,physical-layout's key centres (one or
      the other asserted; src/keys.c), and the
      distance between two keys through an integer square root. Each effect
      that measures the board selects it, the star waves, and the gradient
      picture for the board's ends; nothing to set.

config LANTERN_THIN_RING
    bool "Build the thin ring: a thin ring of the palette's next colour from each pressed key"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect thin-ring (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn
      when the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT):
      while the key LEDs are lit, a ring one key wide spreads from each key
      this half presses (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, from
      the other half's keys too, in across the split) at twelve key units a
      second and fades out over a
      second, so it has crossed the half when it is gone; each press takes
      the palette's next colour, at full brightness (so the build refuses a
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100), and a key under two rings shows the
      stronger one's colour. Needs the keys' places (LANTERN_GEOMETRY: the
      lantern,keys node's coords, or the physical layout's, asserted):
      src/effects/thin_ring.c measures the ring on the board. Default n: a
      consumer builds the effects it wants to pick from.

config LANTERN_DISC
    bool "Build the disc: a disc from each pressed key, brightest at its rim, the palette's colours passing inside"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect disc (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while
      the key LEDs are lit, a rim spreads from each key this half
      presses (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, from the other
      half's keys too, in across the split: the disc then lives long enough
      to cross the whole board) at twelve key units a second; a key it has
      passed lights at
      full brightness (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100) and
      fades back to the picture over half a second, its
      colour running once through the palette's colours in order as it
      fades, so the disc is brightest at its rim, dark behind it, and the
      day's colours pass inside it. A key under two discs shows the stronger
      one's colour. Needs the keys' places, as the thin ring does. Default
      n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_STAIN
    bool "Build the stain: the thin ring dyeing the keys it passes in its colour, until the countdown moves the picture"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_THIN_RING
    help
      The effect stain (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT; the pick's
      thin-ring is the ring without the dye, and this is the ring with it):
      the thin ring (LANTERN_THIN_RING, selected) leaves its colour behind: a
      key within about 4.8 key units of the press, where the ring's crest
      still has 0.6 of its full strength (it fades as it spreads), keeps the
      ring's colour at the picture's brightness from the moment the crest
      reaches it (where two rings have passed, the newer press's colour), so
      the half is painted over in the day's colours as you
      type, each press in the palette's next colour (a ring in from the
      other half, LANTERN_HIT, dyes the keys within its reach too, the
      thumbs first). The dye goes when the
      countdown changes the picture (LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS after the
      last key; the next key brings the picture back and its ring dyes it
      afresh), when the pick changes and whenever the LEDs light; without
      the countdown it stays until the LEDs go dark. Default n, as the thin
      ring.

config LANTERN_CROSS_RING
    bool "Build the crossing ring: a thin ring from each pressed key across the whole board, both halves, in the key's band colour"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    select LANTERN_HIT
    help
      The effect cross-ring (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn
      when the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT):
      while the key LEDs are lit, a ring one key wide spreads from each
      pressed key at twelve key units a second over the whole board: from a
      key of this half out through the other half, and from a key of the
      other half, which the split central relays here (LANTERN_HIT,
      selected: this half and the central need the hit node, on a BLE
      split), in at the thumbs and then the inner columns. The ring keeps
      its strength until it has left the board, a second and a half on
      canon's Imprint, where the thin ring fades within a half; its colour
      is the pressed key's band (the lantern,keys node's slots), so both
      halves draw one press in one colour, at full brightness within the
      frame's cap (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100); a key
      under two rings shows the stronger one's colour. Needs the keys'
      places, as the thin ring does. Default n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_BEAMS
    bool "Build the beams: from each pressed key, light running outward along its row and its column, in the key's band colour"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect beams (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while the
      key LEDs are lit, four beams leave each pressed key at twelve key
      units a second, two along its row and two along its column, each
      four fifths of a key wide and fading out over a second, so the row's
      have left the half well before they are gone (and, where the hit node
      exists, LANTERN_HIT, a press of the other half sends its row's beam
      in across the split: from an inner column it arrives at about half
      strength, from an outer one all but spent, from a thumb nearly at
      full strength). A key is on the row or the column when its y or x is
      within half a key unit of the pressed key's, so canon's thumb
      clusters, half a unit off the bottom row and the inner column, make
      rows and columns of their own, two rows of three and three columns of
      two, and the two clusters share their rows. The colour is the
      pressed key's band (the lantern,keys node's slots), as the crossing
      ring's, so both halves draw one press in one colour, at full
      brightness within the frame's cap (so the build refuses a
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100); a key under two beams shows the stronger
      one's colour. Needs the keys' places, as the thin ring does. Default
      n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_WATER
    bool "Build the water: from each pressed key, waves over the keys, crests toward the palette's first colour and troughs toward its last"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect water (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while the
      key LEDs are lit, each pressed key starts waves, a front spreading at
      twelve key units a second with a wavelength of three key units behind
      it, fading out over a second and a half and weaker the farther from
      the key (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, a press of the
      other half starts waves too, which come in across the split low:
      about a fifth of a crest from an inner column, three fifths from a
      thumb). The waves of every press add up: where two crests meet the
      surface rises higher, where a crest meets a trough it stays still. A
      key is pulled from the picture along the palette's colours by the
      surface's height, a full crest to the first, a full trough to the
      last, a lower wave to the colours between, and as far as the surface
      is from still, so the still surface shows the picture (black with the
      no picture); the pressed key's first waves flicker between the first
      and last colours four times a second, lower waves (later, or farther
      out) between colours nearer the middle; at full brightness within the
      frame's cap (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100).
      zmk-advanced-underglow-effects-vfx's water, from its idea (nothing
      copied). Needs the keys' places, as the thin ring does. Default n,
      as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_DART
    bool "Build the dart: from each pressed key, a bar running across the rows toward the far side of the board, its colour sliding along the palette as it runs"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect dart (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while the
      key LEDs are lit, each pressed key sends a bar across the rows toward
      the far side of the board, at sixteen key units a second (a key left
      of the board's middle sends it right, one right of it left), full at
      its head and gone four fifths of a unit from it, full on the pressed
      key's row and two fifths as strong on the other rows, fading out
      over 1.2 s, in the palette's first colour at the key and sliding
      along the colours as it runs, the last colour a board's width away
      (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, the bar runs on
      through the other half: a press of the other half brings it in
      across the split at about two thirds of its strength from an inner
      column, less by the frame's phase, nearly full from a thumb); at
      full brightness within the
      frame's cap (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100).
      zmk-advanced-underglow-effects-vfx's dart, from its idea (nothing
      copied). Needs the keys' places, as the thin ring does. Default n,
      as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_LAYER_KEYS
    bool "Build the layer keys: while a layer is held, the keys it binds light in its colour"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_LAYER
    help
      The effect layer-keys (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn
      when the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT):
      while the key LEDs are lit and a layer the split central shows is
      held, the keys that layer binds to something of its own (neither
      &none nor &trans, nor the default layer's binding at that key) light
      in its colour, the palette slot the lantern,behavior-layer node's
      layers property gives it, at full brightness within the frame's cap
      (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100); with no shown
      layer held, nothing. The central knows the keymap and its layers,
      a half neither: the central sends each half the keys and the slot
      (LANTERN_LAYER, selected: this half and the central need the layer
      node, on a BLE split), a layer coming into view 60 ms after it does,
      so a short tap of a key that raises a layer does not flash, and no
      layer at once. A press draws nothing; with other effects picked the
      layer keys draw over them and share the frame's cap with them, so
      they dim while a press's effect lives, and a layer of many keys or a
      whitish colour shows dimmer than one of few. A pick's colour paints
      every layer in it. A half shows the layer only while it is lit: one
      gone dark for idle lights at its own next input, as ever. Default n,
      as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_HIT
    bool "Carry each half's key presses to the other half through the split central, for the effects that cross the split"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_HIT_ENABLED
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_BLE
    help
      The split central sends each key press, a half's or its own, to every
      other half as one invocation of the lantern,behavior-hit node, which
      ZMK's split transport carries by the node's name: the node is named
      hit on every device (src/hit.c, the binding), param1 the key position.
      A half notes a relayed press as a hit for its effects while its LEDs
      are lit, as one of its own keys: the crossing ring draws it from the
      other half's key, the beams, the thin ring, the disc, the water and
      the dart do too, in their own colours (the beams' row comes in across
      the split, the disc then lives long enough to cross the board, the
      water's waves come in low, from a thumb higher, the dart's bar on its
      way), the
      stain dyes with it, and it counts in the press count the thin ring's
      colours follow, whichever of them the pick draws; its countdown's
      clock ignores it. The central sends
      only to a half whose link runs at peripheral latency 0, which a half
      asks for while USB powers it (LANTERN_USB_LATENCY, on by default): a
      half on battery is dark, and writes to it would wait up to 31
      connection events each and, in a run, dry the TX pool on which the run
      queue's drain blocks with the lit half's writes behind; a consumer
      that turns LANTERN_USB_LATENCY off on its halves gets no relay. The
      sending runs on the relay's work queue (LANTERN_RELAY), never the
      system work queue, and the hits share the central's run queue with the
      palette (src/relay.c and src/hit.c say why, and what a vanished half
      costs). Default y where the node is present on a BLE split: the node
      is the switch, as the palette's.

config LANTERN_LAYER
    bool "Carry the keymap's layer to the halves through the split central, for the layer keys"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_LAYER_ENABLED
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_BLE
    help
      The split central sends every half the state of the keymap's layer as
      invocations of the lantern,behavior-layer node, one per chunk of 61
      key positions (one for a keymap of up to 61, two up to 122; the
      chunk's number and the last chunk's mark ride in the event position,
      src/layer.h), which ZMK's split transport carries by the node's name:
      the node is named layer on every device (src/layer.c, the binding).
      The state is which keys the layer that shows binds to something of its
      own, and the palette slot of its colour; the node's layers property,
      pairs of a layer and a slot, names the layers that show, the highest
      held one showing and the others passed over, and only the central
      reads it. A half hands the state to its layer keys
      (LANTERN_LAYER_KEYS). The central sends a state when it changes, a
      layer coming into view 60 ms late, and only to a half whose link runs
      at peripheral latency 0, as the hit (LANTERN_HIT), from the relay's
      work queue (LANTERN_RELAY), and again to a half when its link comes to
      latency 0; a half drops its state when its link to the central or its
      USB power goes, and takes none on battery. Nothing is kept in the
      settings. Default y where the node is present on a BLE split: the node
      is the switch, as the hit's.

config LANTERN_RELAY
    bool
    default y if ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL && (LANTERN_HIT || LANTERN_LAYER)
    help
      The split central's work queue for what the module's code sends the
      halves, the hit's presses (LANTERN_HIT) and the layer's state
      (LANTERN_LAYER), and the rule of whom to send to, a half whose link
      runs at peripheral latency 0 (src/relay.c). On by itself with either
      on a split central; nothing to set.

config LANTERN_USB_LATENCY
    bool "Ask the split central for peripheral latency 0 while USB powers this half"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_BLE
    depends on !ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    help
      On a split peripheral whose key LEDs light on USB power: while USB
      powers it, the half asks the central for peripheral latency 0 on
      their link, so what the central sends it (a palette, a relayed key
      press) arrives at the next connection event, not up to 31 events
      later (232.5 ms at ZMK's 7.5 ms interval and latency 30); on battery
      it asks for the latency the link had at connection, the central's,
      so the battery pays nothing. The interval and the timeout stay the
      link's. Zephyr holds a peripheral's request for the first
      CONFIG_BT_CONN_PARAM_UPDATE_TIMEOUT ms of a connection (5 s), and the
      central's host accepts it; src/latency.c says why and how.

config LANTERN_PALETTE
    bool "Keep a palette of six colours, set from the split central's host; the key LEDs show them"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_PALETTE_ENABLED
    depends on SETTINGS
    help
      Six colours (0xRRGGBB, slots 0..5) that the host hands the split
      central (LANTERN_PORT) and that the central and every half keep
      in their settings ("lantern/palette/colours"); a half's key LEDs show
      them in the picture (LANTERN_KEYS; the bands by default), repainted
      when a palette arrives while they are lit and shown at every boot. A
      set of fewer than six colours clears the rest to 0x000000, which the
      LEDs show as slot 0. The central's code invokes the
      lantern,behavior-palette node, which ZMK's split transport carries to
      every half by its name: the node is named palette on every device
      (src/palette.c, the binding), one pair of slots an invocation, three
      a palette. On a split central the palette is resent to a
      half at each of its battery reports, the first of which follows every
      (re)connection, so a half that was away gets it when it is back: that
      needs CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_BATTERY_LEVEL_FETCHING, asserted at
      build time; and its run queue must hold a whole palette for every
      half at once, CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_SPLIT_RUN_QUEUE_SIZE at
      least 3 x the peripheral count (ZMK's default is 5), asserted too, and
      one more a half with each pick (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_INTRO: 10 for
      two halves with both). Default y where the node is present: the node
      is the switch, as the lantern,rail node is for LANTERN_RAIL.

config LANTERN_PORT
    bool "Take palette, effect and intro commands on the split central's USB CDC ACM port"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_PALETTE || LANTERN_EFFECT || LANTERN_INTRO
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    depends on USB_CDC_ACM
    select UART_INTERRUPT_DRIVEN
    help
      Text lines on any CDC ACM port of the split central (src/port.c is the
      whole grammar): "palette RRGGBB [RRGGBB ...]" (one to six colours)
      sets slots 0.. on every half and on the central and clears the rest,
      answered with "palette ok" and the six slots, and "palette" or
      "palette?" asks for them (LANTERN_PALETTE); "effect <name> [<name>
      ...] [RRGGBB]" (src/effect.h's names, and a colour or none for the
      palette's) picks the effects on every half and on the central,
      answered with "effect ok", the names and the colour when set, and
      "effect" or "effect?" asks (LANTERN_EFFECT); "intro <name>" (src/intro.h's names,
      one) picks the intro the boot light plays the same way, answered with
      "intro ok" and the name, and "intro" or "intro?" asks (LANTERN_INTRO).
      canon's scripts/dongle.py palette, effect and intro send them to the
      Imprint Dongle. The port's rate is untouched: 1200 baud is zmk-beacon's
      bootloader entry. A reply is written without waiting for a reader; a
      line that begins with none of the words gets none. It takes the UART
      interrupt callback of every CDC ACM port, of which a device keeps one,
      which is why one file reads every word: a consumer that gives a port
      to a shell or another reader sets this n.

endmenu
